TR_PHY_STATUS -- READ IT, OR SAY WHY NOT
-----------------------------------------

An earlier analysis left this open as "read it, or say why not". Read: it
is PHY_STATUS at 0x0280, UM 15.4.7.38, and the UM defines NO bits -- the field
only defers to a note about whichever PHY is actually used.
There is no such application note in this tree. So a "PHY ready"
predicate built on any bit of it would be an invented predicate, and none
is built. It is read and printed at dev_dbg immediately before TPS1 and
in the `train` file, so the value is on record; nothing branches on it.

ONE VALUE THE UM SETTLED THAT THIS FILE HAD WRONG
--------------------------------------------------

An earlier analysis listed LINK_BW_SET, LANE_COUNT_SET and
ENHANCED_FRAME_EN among the registers the sequence writes. ENHANCED_FRAME_EN
(0x0008) is READ-ONLY: UM 15.4.7.3 describes it as hard-wired
to 1. So the source is in enhanced framing whatever anyone writes, DPCD 0x101
bit 7 is the source DESCRIBING itself rather than choosing, and a sink without
ENHANCED_FRAME_CAP cannot be framed correctly by this transmitter at all. That
gets one dev_warn naming both sides rather than a silent mismatch.

THE DEFECT THE BOARD FOUND IN THIS PATCH, AND WHAT ANSWERS IT
--------------------------------------------------------------

A board run on 2026-08-19, on a fixture that PASSED the acceptance script's
alt-mode preflight -- so this is not a dead-fixture artefact, it is the driver
being wrong about a link that was there. `echo 0 > .../train` returned EXIT 0 and
this driver reported:

    state         : trained at 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes     <- the driver
    attempts      : 1 (CR 1 round(s), EQ 3)

while the sink's own bytes, read through the same file, said:

    DPCD 0x202-207: 00 00 80 00 00 00                     <- the sink
    clock recovery: NOT done
    equalisation  : NOT done
    sink in sync  : no
    symbol errors : lane 0: 329, lanes 1/2/3: 32767 (saturated)

CR and EQ were judged from link status taken DURING training and never asked
again once the training pattern was cleared and the scrambler enabled. That is
a vendor defect this tree must not port, in a worse form:
the vendor discards a return value, this discarded the question. The table above
now carries the row.

ANSWERED BY sun60i_dp_train_verify(), which runs after sun60i_dp_train_stop()
and before success is returned. It reads DPCD 0x202..0x207 once with
drm_dp_dpcd_read_link_status() and requires drm_dp_clock_recovery_ok() AND
drm_dp_channel_eq_ok() -- i.e. CR_DONE on every active lane, CHANNEL_EQ_DONE and
SYMBOL_LOCKED on every active lane, and INTERLANE_ALIGN_DONE. Both predicates
are the core's; neither is open-coded. That is the shell instrument's
LANE0_1=0x77 LANE2_3=0x77 ALIGN=0x01 bar in the core's own terms.

ONE READ, NO RETRY. A second look after a failed first one would turn "this link
does not hold once training stops" into "it held the second time", which is the
question, not noise to average away.

And `train`'s `state` line is now built from that reading instead of from
dp->trained. The driver's own flag is still printed -- it is what tells an A/B
run apart from a shell-trained link -- but under its own label, below the sink's
verdict, and labelled as a claim.

IS IT A MISSING CHECK, OR AN ENABLE-ORDER DEFECT? BOTH, AND THE EVIDENCE SAYS
WHICH IS WHICH
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Worth answering rather than assuming, because "the link loses lock the moment
scrambling is enabled" is the kind of symptom that gets a retry bolted onto it.

⚠ It is NOT the ordering inside sun60i_dp_train_stop(). That sequence -- source
TRAINING_PATTERN_SET to 0, settle, DPCD 0x102 to 0, then source
SCRAMBLING_DISABLE to 0 -- is step for step what the by-hand shell script
does, on the runs that produced 77 77 01 01 and a picture
(the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-18 and 2026-08-19). Rearranging ordering that has silicon behind
it, to fix a symptom it demonstrably does not cause, is exactly the unforced
deviation this branch already had to take back once. It stays as it is.

⚠ What the driver omits is the step the instrument takes IMMEDIATELY afterwards,
and this tree already recorded why it is load-bearing. `drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` (on main, HW-proven)
says it in its own header and again in the code comment above
sun60i_dp_video_stream_enable():

    "with training cleared and the stream not enabled the core sends idle and
     the sink loses the lock it just gained, which is what happened here on the
     first successful training, 2026-08-18"

`echo 0 > train` ends in precisely that state. So the sink's 00 00 80 00 00 00
is not only the fingerprint of a missing check -- it is also the documented
resting state of an unfinished sequence, and a check that ignored that would
have replaced one wrong verdict with the opposite one.

Which is why SINK=0x01 -- the fourth number of the instrument's bar -- is NOT
required by sun60i_dp_train_verify(). DPCD 0x205 RECEIVE_PORT_0_STATUS describes
the sink's synchronisation to a received STREAM, not the outcome of training.
Mainline polls it for 1 only after the video stream starts and back to 0 after
it stops -- edp_receiver_ready_T7() and _T9(), the eDP stream-on / stream-off
timings, drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/protocols/link_edp_panel_control.c
:480 and :455 -- and no DisplayPort driver in 6.18.36 gates training on it; the
only other readers of 0x205 take the DP 2.0 INTRA_HOP bit
(drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_dp_link_training.c:1109,
.../link/protocols/link_dp_training.c:1037). i915 says the rest out loud at
intel_dp_link_training.c:1127: what a source transmits after training "is
platform specific ... on older platforms it's HW specific if/how an idle pattern
is generated".

So the bit is required exactly where it IS defined, which is two places and not
none:

  - in sun60i_dp_train_verify() itself WHEN A STREAM IS ALREADY RUNNING
    (dp->video_on) -- the re-training case, which is what a KMS driver will do on
    an IRQ_HPD link-status change. There the fourth number of the bar is
    meaningful at the end of training, and it is required.
  - in sun60i_dp_video_stream_enable(), which now waits for it, bounded, and
    refuses to report the stream started until the sink says it is receiving it.

Bounded POLLING, not a retry -- nothing is re-driven between reads, and the wait
is the eDP T7 allowance mainline polls for in the same shape (the by-hand
sequence waits 500 ms before it reads). The stream is left enabled on failure,
because the next thing a human does with a black screen is read the registers.

The only case where the bit is not asked for is the first training of a link that
has no stream behind it yet, and there it is skipped with a dev_dbg line saying
so rather than silently.

⬜ ONE THING THIS DOES NOT SETTLE, named rather than buried: whether the link
bits (CR / EQ / symbol lock / interlane align) hold on this core's idle at all.
If they do not, sun60i_dp_train_verify() will refuse a link that trained
perfectly, and the answer is that `train` and the stream cannot be separate verbs
on this part. That is a sharp, falsifiable outcome and the acceptance below asks
for it directly -- it is not something to pre-empt with a workaround.

⬜ And one residue of the proven order, recorded rather than changed: between the
DPCD 0x102 write and the source's SCRAMBLING_DISABLE write, the sink descrambles
a source that is still sending unscrambled output -- one AUX transaction wide.
The instrument has the same window and survives it. Falsifier for it mattering:
symbol-error counters that are non-zero at the end of a link that otherwise
reads 0x77/0x77/0x01/0x01.

WHAT THIS CANNOT DO TODAY, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED
------------------------------------------------------

⚠ The rate ladder is one rung long. The PHY has exactly one PMA rate table,
2700 Mb/s (`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`); every other rung is refused by phy_validate() and never
entered. So RATE fallback is plumbing waiting for a second table -- only the LANE
fallback (4 -> 2 -> 1) can actually change anything today. What a second rate
would need is a second 163-word PMA table in the PHY, its own reset-bracket run,
and its own swing table; none of that is here and none of it is guessed at.

Rate before lanes, which keeps bandwidth: four lanes at a lower rate carry more
than two lanes at the same rate. The exception is an EQ-only failure -- clock
recovery proved out, so the clock is fine and the problem is inter-lane, which
fewer lanes can fix and a lower rate need not. That case skips the rest of the
rate ladder and drops the lane count, which is why train_eq() returns -EPROTO
there and -ENOLINK when CR was lost. hibmc_dp_link_training() makes the same
distinction (dp_link.c:310-338).

The rungs are rebuilt when the lane count drops, because a PHY may allow a rate
at two lanes that it refuses at four -- and phy_validate() is asked before each
attempt rather than after tearing a working link down to find out. The vendor
maps both -EOPNOTSUPP and -EINVAL from phy_validate() to "pass"
(sunxi_drm_edp.c:846-868), so its one real rejection is discarded again.

THE INTERFACE
-------------

A `train` debugfs file beside `link` and `video`, for the same reason those two
are debugfs: this tree has no KMS driver and no HPD path into one, so there is
nothing else to call sun60i_dp_train(). Writing "<rate Mb/s> [lanes]" trains
exactly that; writing "0" walks the ladder. READING it asks the SINK -- the
`state` line included, and everything below it, all of it back over AUX from
DPCD 0x202..0x207 and 0x210.., not from a register of ours reading back.

    state         : sink confirms the link at 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes
    driver claim  : trained (this driver's own flag; not evidence)

Two lines, deliberately, and in that order. They agree on a healthy link and
they are the two halves of the A/B: a link the sink confirms while the driver
does not claim it was trained by the by-hand shell script.

⚠ The symbol-error counters are printed with their VALID bit, not just their
count: bit 15 clear means the sink is not counting, which is not the same as
zero errors, and a reader that prints only the count reports a perfect link on a
sink that has none.

sun60i_dp_video_stream_enable() WARNS when the link was not trained by this
driver rather than refusing. That is deliberate and temporary:
the by-hand shell script is still the A/B arm until this patch is
HW-proven, and refusing would make the two impossible to compare on one kernel.
The warning is what tells the two runs apart in a log.

WHAT IT OWES
------------

Every item is a reading on the 8 GiB board with a DP sink on J4, on a kernel carrying
`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-lane-map-and-inverts` + `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis` + this patch. Driven by a shell script, which now
REFUSES to run unless DP alt mode is genuinely entered -- see the gate below.

  0. THE FIXTURE IS ALIVE, and this is a gate rather than a check because on
     2026-08-19 it was not: a Type-C recovery had left DP alt mode, the four
     COMB0 lanes were in reset, and a full page of confident-looking failures
     came out of a dead fixture. A DPCD read succeeding does NOT establish alt
     mode -- AUX runs over the SBU pair through its own PHY (`phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy`) and answers
     perfectly well with the main link in reset. The gate asked TCPM alone
     (`port0-partner.0/active`), which answers "did the partner enter an alt
     mode", not "are the four COMB0 lanes carrying DisplayPort". It now needs
     three witnesses, all required: TCPM's `active` = yes, the combo-PHY's own
     handover line in dmesg ("DP alt mode pin assignment <C|E>: handing all four
     COMB0 lanes to DisplayPort", with no later "DP alt mode left"), and
     `dp_active : 1` in the combo-PHY's debugfs `state`.
     FALSIFIED BY: any of the three missing -- in which case nothing below this
     line means anything and the script exits 2.
     ⚠ RECOVERY IS A POWER CYCLE. The unbind/bind of the rt1711h i2c device is
     NOT a safe recovery: its second use on 2026-08-19 LEFT DP alt mode outright
     ("DP alt mode left, COMB0 links back in reset") and the kernel then wedged
     with RCU stalls on one CPU until the board was power-cycled anyway.

  1. THE LINK TRAINS FROM THE DRIVER, AND THE SINK AGREES.
     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/train
     Expected: exit 0, dmesg "link trained: 2700 Mb/s over 4 lanes, CR in 1
     round(s), EQ in 1", and `cat train` showing
     "state         : sink confirms the link", "driver claim  : trained",
     clock recovery done, equalisation done, four symbol-error counters valid
     at 0. "sink in sync" reads NO here and that is correct -- it is a stream
     bit; item 3 is where it counts.
     FALSIFIED BY: a non-zero exit; "PHY refused the drive levels" (`phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis` not in
     the kernel); "the sink does not confirm ... once the training pattern is
     cleared", which is the loop believing itself; CR completing only after
     several rounds where the shell script does it in one on the same cable and
     monitor (the drive levels this writes would then not be the ones the script
     writes).

  2. THE DRIVE LEVELS REACHED THE PMA. TX_TXCC_MGNFS_MULT_000, lanes 0..3, at
     0x06C880A0 / 0x06C884A0 / 0x06C888A0 / 0x06C88CA0 -- that is
     0x06C80000 + ((0x4000 + lane*0x200 + 0x50) << 1), the address
     a733_combophy_dp_set_voltages() writes and the one `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`'s rate table names
     as 0x4050/0x4250/0x4450/0x4650.
     Expected: each inside 0x0009..0x0028, the band every legal non-maximum cell
     of `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis`'s table occupies, with both 16-bit halves equal.
     FALSIFIED BY: 0x002A, which is what `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-rate-2700`'s rate table leaves, i.e.
     set_voltages never ran; 0x0000, which is either no write at all or a link
     resting at maximum drive; anything outside the band, which no cell produces.

  3. A PICTURE, from the drivers only, and the sink says it is receiving it.
     echo configure > .../video ; echo 0 > .../train ; echo stream > .../video
     Expected: `echo stream` exits 0 -- which now means the sink reported
     RECEIVE_PORT_0_STATUS within the bounded wait -- a 1920x1200 test pattern on
     the screen, `cat train` showing "sink in sync  : yes" AND still
     "state         : sink confirms the link", and NO "the main link was not
     trained by this driver" line in dmesg.
     FALSIFIED BY: "the sink does not report itself in sync with the stream
     after N ms"; a black screen with every register right, which is what
     happened on 2026-08-18 and is why nothing here replaces the eye.

  4. THE SHELL SCRIPT IS NO LONGER NEEDED -- and the A/B arm proves it. Same
     kernel, same board, once with the driver's `train` and once with
     the shell script given 4 and 2700: both must give 77 77 01 01.
     FALSIFIED BY: the script training a link the driver cannot.

  5. LANE FALLBACK, the only ladder arm that can move today. Force it:
     echo "2700 4" > train on a two-lane pin assignment, or unplug two lanes.
     Expected: the attempt at 4 fails, the driver retries at 2, and `train`
     reports 2 lanes with attempts >= 2.
     FALSIFIED BY: the driver stopping at the first failure.

  6. THE FIVE-TRIES RULE ACTUALLY BOUNDS. With no sink able to lock (a
     deliberately bad cable), training must return in bounded time and log
     "attempts at an unchanged drive level" or "every lane at maximum swing",
     not hang.
     FALSIFIED BY: an unbounded loop, or a single attempt.

  7. HELD. All of items 1 and 3 unchanged over minutes, not sampled once.

  8. ⬜ THE OPEN QUESTION THIS PATCH DELIBERATELY DOES NOT PRE-EMPT: do the link
     bits hold on this core's idle? Item 1 answers it either way. If `train`
     alone reports "the sink does not confirm ... once the training pattern is
     cleared" while item 3 then produces a picture and 77 77 01 01, the answer
     is no -- the core's idle does not hold the sink -- and `train` and `stream`
     cannot be separate verbs on this part. That is a design change to make with
     the measurement in hand, not a retry to add now.

Explicitly NOT proof: a green build, the `train` file existing, or the driver's
own `trained` flag -- which is why every number the file prints, `state`
included, comes from the sink.

WHICH OF THE THREE IS HARDWARE-PROVEN, PLAINLY
-----------------------------------------------

  `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-lane-map-and-inverts`  ✅ HW-PROVEN, the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-19. Lane map 0x00003210 at 0x06C06C24,
        lane invert 0xf, with the debug instrumentation never touched (`forced: no`), so the
        values reach the production path from the mux callback.
  `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-swing-and-pre-emphasis`  ⬜ NOT proven, in either direction. Nothing in that session exercised it.
  `drm-a733-dp-link-training`  ⬜ NOT proven -- and its first cut was REFUTED by that run: it reported a
        link the sink denied, on a fixture whose alt-mode preflight had passed.
        That defect is fixed above.

Whether the CR/EQ loop can train a HEALTHY link is still neither proven nor
refuted. The run answered "does the driver tell the truth about the link" (it did
not, and now it must) and left "can this loop train a link" open: the attempts
made after the Type-C recovery could not answer it, because that recovery had
left DP alt mode ("DP alt mode left, COMB0 links back in reset") and the lanes
were in reset. The same failure reproduced on a kernel WITHOUT these
patches, which rules the patches out as its cause.

⚠ RECOVERY IS A POWER CYCLE. The unbind/bind of the rt1711h i2c device is not a
safe recovery: its second use on 2026-08-19 left DP alt mode outright and the
kernel then wedged with RCU stalls on CPU 7 until the board was power-cycled
anyway. Item 0 of the acceptance now refuses to run in that state instead of
measuring it.

Build check: the series applies at --fuzz=0 on a freshly unpacked 6.18.36 tree,
drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ builds clean at W=1, checkpatch 0/0.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -14,9 +14,10 @@
  * The main link is the second, and it is a PHY consumer's job: COMB0 belongs to
  * the combo-PHY driver, and a DisplayPort controller reaches it through
  * phy_configure() with a union phy_configure_opts_dp, the way every DP
- * controller in mainline does. What is still absent is link training and video:
- * no training pattern sequence, no DPCD 0x100/0x102 writes, no mode setting, no
- * KMS.
+ * controller in mainline does. Link training is the third, and with it the last
+ * piece of the pixel path that was still a shell script. What is still absent
+ * is KMS: no connector, no CRTC, no modeset -- the mode is a built-in constant
+ * and the verbs are debugfs files.
  *
  * Every register offset and field below comes from the UM's own register list
  * (chapter 15.4.7 of the A733 user manual) and
@@ -64,6 +65,7 @@
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 #include <linux/sprintf.h>
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
+#include <linux/unaligned.h>
 
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h>
@@ -141,6 +143,7 @@
 static const unsigned int sun60i_dp_link_rates[] = { 810000, 540000, 270000, 162000 };
 
 #define SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(r)	((unsigned int)(r) / 100)
+#define SUN60I_DP_MBPS_TO_RATE(m)	((int)(m) * 100)
 
 /*
  * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -296,6 +299,16 @@ struct sun60i_dp {
 	bool			dpcd_valid;
 	unsigned int		link_rate;
 	unsigned int		link_lanes;
+	/* Link training. `trained` is this driver's own answer and therefore not
+	 * evidence -- the sink's answer is DPCD 0x202..0x205 and the `train`
+	 * file reads it every time it is opened. The three counters exist
+	 * because "it trained" and "it trained on the first attempt" are
+	 * different results and only the second one is a healthy link.
+	 */
+	bool			trained;
+	unsigned int		train_attempts;
+	unsigned int		train_cr_rounds;
+	unsigned int		train_eq_rounds;
 	/* The main video stream. The second reg window is eDP_REG_PAD at
